 ## Overview
-GASP is a pipeline that integrates 3D Gaussian Splatting with black-box physics engines by converting flat Gaussian components into triangle faces, simulating the vertices using the Material Point Method (MPM), and reconstructing Gaussians from the deformed triangles [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of simulating deformable objects in Gaussian representations without requiring modifications to the physics engine or complex mesh extraction strategies like marching cubes [S1, unofficial]. Unlike PhysGaussian, which approximates deformation via first-order linear transformations, GASP operates directly on the geometry of flat Gaussians using the GaMeS parameterization, ensuring stable simulations and avoiding artifacts associated with treating points as discrete entities [S1, unofficial].
+GASP is a pipeline that integrates 3D Gaussian Splatting with black-box physics engines by converting flat Gaussian components into triangle faces, simulating the vertices using the Material Point Method (MPM), and reconstructing Gaussians from the deformed triangles [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of simulating deformable objects in Gaussian representations without requiring modifications to the physics engine or complex mesh extraction strategies like marching cubes [S1, unofficial]. Unlike PhysGaussian, which approximates deformation via first-order linear transformations and requires adjustments to the physics engine, GASP operates directly on the geometry of flat Gaussians using the GaMeS parameterization, ensuring stable simulations and avoiding artifacts associated with treating points as discrete entities [S1, unofficial].
 
